<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Czech Silesia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Czech Silesia is the part of the historical region of Silesia that lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a>. It descends from the small part of Silesia that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> kept after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> conquered most of the region in 1742, ruled as Austrian Silesia until 1918. In 1928 it was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-silesian-land">Moravian-Silesian Land</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/czech-silesia.jpg" alt="Czech Silesia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michal Klajban · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamenit%C3%BD_(Moravskoslezsk%C3%A9_Beskydy).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Czech Silesia has no formal boundaries. In a narrow geographic sense it covers most or all of the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> that lies in the drainage basin of the Oder. It lies in the north-east of the country, mainly in the Moravian-Silesian Region, with a section in the north of the Olomouc Region, and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> in the south-east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a> stands roughly at its centre, and after it the most important towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> and Český Těšín.</p><p>About two thirds of the territory lies in the Eastern Sudetes, and the rest reaches into the Ostrava Basin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-gate">Moravian Gate</a> and the western Western Beskids. Its main rivers are the Oder, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a>, which forms part of the natural border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Middle Ages Silesia belonged to Piast-ruled Poland and in the 14th century passed gradually to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-silesian-war">First Silesian War</a> in 1742 the rest of Silesia was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and the part that stayed within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy was reorganised as the Duchy of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a>. In 1900 it covered 5,140 square kilometres and had 670,000 inhabitants.</p><p>In 1918 it became part of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Cieszyn Silesia was divided between Czechoslovakia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1920, and the Hlučín Region, formerly Prussian, joined Czechoslovakia by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of 1938 most of the area was joined to the Reichsgau Sudetenland, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> occupied the Trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a> area for a time. During the German occupation Polish activists, priests and teachers were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> in 1939 and 1940, and the Germans ran forced labour camps and prisoner-of-war camps in the region.</p><p>Until 1945 most of Czech Silesia, apart from the districts around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a> and Hlučín, was settled by German speakers. After the war the ethnic Germans were expelled under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a>, and the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was set again along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the population speaks Czech with altered vowels. Some of the Slavic population speak Lach, which is classed as a dialect of Czech but resembles Polish in places, and in Cieszyn Silesia a separate Cieszyn Silesian dialect is spoken, mostly by the Polish minority. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, Czech Silesia is one of the three historical Czech lands. Its notable natives include the biologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a>, the politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-kudlich">Hans Kudlich</a> and the astronomer Johann Palisa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Czech Silesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the historic rights of the Bohemian Crown became the basis of Czech national claims, articulated by the historian František Palacký and the Czech National Revival, and the historic frontiers of Bohemia, Moravia and <b>Czech Silesia</b> still largely form the borders of the Czech Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1928 Moravia was merged with <b>Czech Silesia</b> into the Moravian-Silesian Land, which the communist regime dissolved in 1949 when it replaced the historic lands with regions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
